// epistemic.dl -- how epistemic state combines along a derivation.
//
// A conclusion is never better warranted than the weakest step used to reach
// it. Composing two facts therefore yields whichever input carries the weaker
// warrant.
//
// The weakening order is part of the rules.v2 bundle contract:
//
//   MUST  <  MAY  <  INFERRED  <  ASSUMED  <  UNKNOWN
//
// MUST is observed and definite. MAY is observed as possible. INFERRED is
// derived from analysed evidence. ASSUMED rests on an external model that
// nothing in this run verified, so it ranks weaker than INFERRED. UNKNOWN
// carries no information at all.
//
// MUST_NOT (2) is deliberately absent. It is a negative statement and never
// participates in a positive derivation; a rule that encounters it produces
// no tuple rather than silently coercing it to a positive state. Changing any
// of this changes published fact content and requires a bundle version bump.

.decl EpistemicRank(state:Epistemic, rank:number)
EpistemicRank(0, 0).  // MUST
EpistemicRank(1, 1).  // MAY
EpistemicRank(3, 2).  // INFERRED
EpistemicRank(4, 3).  // ASSUMED
EpistemicRank(5, 4).  // UNKNOWN

// Total over every state the bundle admits. Declaring it by rank rather than
// enumerating 25 tuples keeps the order in one place.
.decl WeakenEpistemic(left:Epistemic, right:Epistemic, result:Epistemic)
WeakenEpistemic(l, r, l) :-
  EpistemicRank(l, lr), EpistemicRank(r, rr), lr >= rr.
WeakenEpistemic(l, r, r) :-
  EpistemicRank(l, lr), EpistemicRank(r, rr), rr > lr.
